  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x² + z²)/2, drawn at half its value over x, z from -2 to 2. It is steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first; plain descent diverges along x once the learning rate exceeds 0.8.",
    "Each step moves by -(learning rate) × (true slope + noise). Noise per direction is Gaussian with spread 2·sqrt((64-b)/(63b)) for batch size b: 2 at b=1, exactly 0 at b=64. With noise a step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are drawn 2.5 times their true step length, the same factor for both. Orange: the actual next step (against the noisy estimate). Grey: learning rate × true downhill slope.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or is marked diverged when the loss exceeds 5 or the ball leaves the drawn surface; positions off the surface are drawn at its edge.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed: Reset replays the same noise; each new run uses the next noise values. Five steps per second at normal speed."
  ]
}